             "  Ac    →  Carrier amplitude\n"
             "  μ     →  Modulation index  (0 ≤ μ ≤ 1)\n"
             "  fm    →  Message (modulating) signal frequency\n"
             "  fc    →  Carrier signal frequency"),

            ("📶  Carrier Signal",
             "c(t) = Ac · cos(2π·fc·t)\n\n"
             "The carrier is a high-frequency sinusoid whose amplitude, frequency, or phase "
             "is altered by the message signal. In AM, only the amplitude is varied. A typical "
             "carrier might be in the MHz range for commercial radio."),

            ("🎵  Message Signal",
             "m(t) = Am · cos(2π·fm·t)\n\n"
             "The message (modulating) signal carries the information to be transmitted. It "
             "is generally a low-frequency signal — for audio, typically 20 Hz – 20 kHz."),

            ("📊  Modulation Index  μ",
             "μ  =  Am / Ac\n\n"
             "• 0  < μ < 1  →  Under-modulation  (normal, no distortion)\n"
             "• μ = 1       →  Full modulation     (maximum efficiency without distortion)\n"
             "• μ > 1       →  Over-modulation     (causes distortion and envelope inversion)\n\n"
             "Efficiency  η  =  μ² / (2 + μ²)  ×  100 %\n"
             "At μ = 1, maximum efficiency = 33.3 %"),

            ("📡  Sidebands & Bandwidth",
             "Expanding the AM equation yields three frequency components:\n\n"
             "  1) Carrier        :  fc          (amplitude = Ac)\n"
             "  2) Upper Sideband :  fc + fm     (amplitude = μ·Ac / 2)\n"
             "  3) Lower Sideband :  fc − fm     (amplitude = μ·Ac / 2)\n\n"
             "Bandwidth  BW  =  2 × fm\n\n"
             "All information is contained in the sidebands. The carrier carries no "
             "information but consumes significant power."),

            ("⚡  Power Relations",
             "Carrier Power    Pc  =  Ac² / 2\n"
             "Sideband Power   Ps  =  (μ²/2) · Pc       (total of both sidebands)\n"
             "Total Power      Pt  =  Pc · (1 + μ²/2)\n\n"
